WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.306
Hello.

2
00:00:04.896 --> 00:00:07.984
My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry.

3
00:00:09.574 --> 00:00:11.013
I am an architect.

4
00:00:12.603 --> 00:00:15.866
Ten years of architectural experience.

5
00:00:17.456 --> 00:00:18.942
Episode 2.

6
00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:21.879
Types of software.

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.892
Quick quiz.

8
00:00:36.482 --> 00:00:39.478
What kind of software is Kubernetes?

9
00:00:41.068 --> 00:00:43.100
Here is the answer the chapter gives

10
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.087
it is system software

11
00:00:45.507 --> 00:00:47.190
delivered as a platform

12
00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.848
and it is not itself a microservice.

13
00:00:53.438 --> 00:00:55.621
Three answers to one question

14
00:00:56.041 --> 00:01:00.719
because the question ‘what kind of software is this’ is really four questions in disguise.

15
00:01:01.819 --> 00:01:05.198
There is no single list of software types.

16
00:01:06.788 --> 00:01:09.760
A useful classification says which question it is answering.

17
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.801
Four questions cover what this research needs.

18
00:01:16.391 --> 00:01:19.166
What job does the program do beside the hardware?

19
00:01:20.526 --> 00:01:22.871
What kind of problem is it built to solve?

20
00:01:24.231 --> 00:01:26.541
How does a customer receive it?

21
00:01:28.131 --> 00:01:31.220
And how is a business application structured?

22
00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:37.036
One program occupies one place on each cut.

23
00:01:38.586 --> 00:01:39.956
The everyday cut.

24
00:01:41.546 --> 00:01:43.624
System software operates the machine

25
00:01:44.044 --> 00:01:45.356
an operating system

26
00:01:45.776 --> 00:01:47.204
a container runtime

27
00:01:47.624 --> 00:01:49.110
a database engine

28
00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:50.575
Kubernetes.

29
00:01:51.715 --> 00:01:53.270
Middleware carries messages

30
00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:54.550
requests

31
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.709
and identity between programs

32
00:01:58.129 --> 00:01:59.360
a message broker

33
00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:00.999
an API gateway

34
00:02:01.419 --> 00:02:02.569
a service mesh.

35
00:02:03.709 --> 00:02:06.727
Application software does a job somebody asked for

36
00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:08.389
the order service

37
00:02:08.809 --> 00:02:10.191
the delivery service

38
00:02:10.611 --> 00:02:12.353
the product a tenant logs into.

39
00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:17.672
And development software builds, tests, and releases the other three.

40
00:02:18.772 --> 00:02:23.056
Roger Pressman’s textbook groups programs by the problem they solve

41
00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.591
system software

42
00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.508
application software

43
00:02:26.928 --> 00:02:28.682
engineering and scientific

44
00:02:29.102 --> 00:02:29.879
embedded

45
00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:31.286
product-line

46
00:02:31.706 --> 00:02:32.902
web applications

47
00:02:33.322 --> 00:02:35.354
and artificial-intelligence software.

48
00:02:36.494 --> 00:02:38.502
Keep that last one in mind.

49
00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:42.089
It is going to matter.

50
00:02:43.639 --> 00:02:47.749
The third cut is the one the US standards body NIST made for cloud computing

51
00:02:48.169 --> 00:02:49.737
plus the older packaged form.

52
00:02:50.877 --> 00:02:54.627
On-premises: a copy you install and operate.

53
00:02:56.217 --> 00:03:00.408
Infrastructure as a service: machines you fill.

54
00:03:01.998 --> 00:03:05.376
Platform as a service: your application, on their runtime.

55
00:03:06.516 --> 00:03:10.080
Software as a service: a login and your data.

56
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.357
Here is the sentence to keep

57
00:03:13.777 --> 00:03:17.318
the delivery type does not change the problem type.

58
00:03:18.908 --> 00:03:23.180
It changes who patches the servers and who onboards the next customer.

59
00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.985
The fourth cut is the subject of the whole series

60
00:03:27.405 --> 00:03:28.218
monolith

61
00:03:28.638 --> 00:03:29.880
modular monolith

62
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.496
microservices

63
00:03:31.916 --> 00:03:34.354
self-contained systems

64
00:03:34.774 --> 00:03:36.237
serverless functions.

65
00:03:37.377 --> 00:03:39.606
And every cell in this grid is real.

66
00:03:41.196 --> 00:03:43.646
A monolith can be SaaS.

67
00:03:45.236 --> 00:03:49.624
A microservice suite can be packaged and run on a company’s own machines.

68
00:03:50.764 --> 00:03:55.107
Kubernetes and a PaaS host whichever structure the team chose.

69
00:03:56.247 --> 00:03:59.161
They are not themselves one of these structures.

70
00:04:00.711 --> 00:04:01.860
Now the payoff.

71
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:09.441
Pressman’s artificial-intelligence category is software whose job is recognition, search, or learned judgment.

72
00:04:10.581 --> 00:04:14.122
The research question is different, and easy to blur with it.

73
00:04:15.262 --> 00:04:20.614
An AI feature inside the order service is Pressman’s category living inside an application.

74
00:04:21.754 --> 00:04:27.037
An AI tool that proposes service boundaries is development software acting on the structure cut.

75
00:04:28.177 --> 00:04:29.651
Two different things.

76
00:04:31.241 --> 00:04:34.631
Naming which cut moved is the discipline of the later chapters.

77
00:04:35.731 --> 00:04:37.832
Three things to carry away.

78
00:04:39.422 --> 00:04:40.049
One.

79
00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:45.355
Four cuts, four questions; one program sits in one cell of each.

80
00:04:46.495 --> 00:04:46.982
Two.

81
00:04:48.572 --> 00:04:56.188
Kubernetes and a PaaS are system software that hosts a structure; they are not a structure.

82
00:04:57.778 --> 00:04:58.661
Three.

83
00:05:00.251 --> 00:05:05.057
The research is about the structure cut — not about Pressman’s AI category.

84
00:05:06.607 --> 00:05:08.360
Next: the history.

85
00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.806
Nobody invented microservices on a given day

86
00:05:13.226 --> 00:05:14.863
so where did they come from?
